 "says": "Alice binds her laptop key and registers a private notes source, then submits records the owner must refuse at admission, each refused with exactly the code and pointer its contract states and nothing written: a key binding that names its own binding_id (runtime_owned_field /binding_id, a C01 kind), a revision that dates itself (runtime_owned_field /produced_at, C01), a package envelope that names Alice's enrolment binding as its trust yet carries the key it asks to be trusted by (unknown_field /package_public_key, C09), a commit request carrying an access token (unknown_field /access_token, C03), and a backup set naming one member twice (duplicate_item /members/4, C03). Also submitted: an operation query naming a schema version this reader does not hold, refused with unsupported_schema_version at /schema. Then a revision signed with her key is altered after signing and committed with that signature among its proofs: the owner verifies the signature against the altered bytes and refuses with signature_invalid at /proof_digests/0. Querying two of the admission-refused request ids returns request_not_held, and committing the revision exactly as signed, still expecting the head the registration made, is accepted — so no refused submission stored a record or moved the head. What must not happen: a refused record stored, a refusal with another code or pointer, or the altered bytes accepted under the original signature.",
 "spec_sha256": "79e0ffd2ae6ca9b31574d3a1638b05ad0a9bb972a1323cf81dcb356477a43a5c",
